LCOM4 counts the class’s responsibilities by looking at its methods and members and
grouping together ones that are connected. (Kind of like the six degrees of Kevin
Bacon, except that you don’t care how many degrees there are.)
All class members used in a given method are seen as being connected to both the
method itself and to each other. If another method uses those same fields (or some
subset of them), then it’s connected to those members and to the first method as well;
it’s added to the relation connection. If it uses totally different members, it forms a
new, unrelated connection with those other members. Methods can be also consid-
ered connected if one calls another and vice versa.
To sum up, two methods (m1 and m2) are grouped together if at least one of the fol-
lowing is true:
Method m1 invokes method m2, or method m2 invokes method m1.
Both methods (m1 and m2) use at least one of the same class attributes

